From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Fri, 10 Oct 2026 19:30:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Work out a complex relocation expression at link time

MACRO-11 writes a complex relocation expression (RLD entry type 017)
for an expression that goes beyond + and - over a relocatable or
external symbol: multiplication, division, the logical operators, a
complement, two relocatable symbols in one expression.  No ELF
relocation can stand for one -- R_PDP11_16 is a symbol plus an addend,
with nowhere to put the arithmetic -- so until now the reader skipped
such an entry, said so, and refused the section's relocations outright,
which is to say the module could not be linked.

It can be worked out instead, and the linker is the one place that can
do it: the expression is arithmetic over addresses, and addresses are
final only once sections have been placed.  So the reader keeps the
expression -- its string of operation codes and the place in the
section whose word it computes -- and bfd_obj_pdp11_resolve_complex(),
declared in the new bfd/obj-pdp11.h, evaluates it and writes the word
into the section contents the linker is about to copy out.

The evaluator follows table 2-7 of the RT-11 Volume and File Formats
Manual to the letter: reverse Polish over a stack, operands 16-bit and
two's complement, the result truncated from the left, a division by
zero yielding zero with a warning, and the PC displacement -- the
difference between the two store operations, 012 and 013 -- applied to
the result only, never to an operand.  A global operand (016) is looked
up in the link's hash table, a p-sect operand (017) names its p-sect by
the place it has in the GSD, which is undocumented but what MACRO-11
and LINK both do.

The hook is finish, in the new emultempl/pdp11rt11cplx.em, shared by
all three rt11 emulations -- sourced by the two that have an
EXTRA_EM_FILE of their own, named as the EXTRA_EM_FILE of the one that
does not.  lang_process() calls it after lang_size_sections() and the
final lang_do_assignments(), so addresses and symbols are final and the
hash table is alive, and before ldwrite(), so the words it writes are
still ahead of the copy that puts them in the output.  The reader goes
on refusing a section's relocations until this has run, so a tool that
is not a link -- objcopy -- cannot quietly produce a file in which the
expression was never computed; and relocatable output, which would have
to carry the expression over rather than resolve it, is refused with
its own message.

Checked against DEC's own LINK, on the expression RT-11's MACRO
produces for `BUF+<N-1>*2' (which in MACRO-11, where there is no
operator precedence, means (BUF+N-1)*2):

    LINK V05.45:  (BUF+01747)*2  = 06076,  minus 01022+2  -> 05052
    this ld:      (OBUF+01747)*2 = 075356, minus 032052+2 -> 043302

each matching the word in its own image to the bit.  Also checked by
running the arithmetic: a module whose code loads through a displaced
complex expression and whose data word is a plain one links and runs on
the machine, reading the right word (0202) and storing the right
address; an undefined global in an expression is named and the link
fails; a division by zero warns and yields zero; `a/2' over a
relocatable symbol comes out exactly half of it. Nothing else moved:
DEC's SYSLIB.OBJ reads as before, a plain MACRO-11 module still links
and runs, and hello, math, fileio, rdwr, lseek and float all pass.
